What is PJM?
PJM Interconnection is the largest electricity grid operator in the U.S., managing the power system across 13 states (from New Jersey to Illinois) plus D.C., serving about 67 million people. It runs wholesale electricity markets and, critically, a capacity market — essentially an auction where PJM pays power plants to promise they’ll be available when demand peaks. Think of it as a reservation fee to keep the lights on.

The Big Story: Data Centers Are Rewriting the Demand Picture
The defining theme in PJM right now is an unprecedented surge in forecasted electricity demand, driven almost entirely by AI data centers.
How big is the shift? Back in PJM’s 2021 load forecast, summer peak demand was projected to grow at about 0.3% per year. The 2026 forecast now projects 3.6% annual growth over the next decade — more than ten times the prior rate.  PJM’s summer peak is now forecasted to reach roughly 222,000 MW by 2036, an increase of nearly 66,000 MW over 10 years.  To put that in context, 66,000 MW is roughly equivalent to the entire generating fleet of a country like Australia.
